TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Executive Summary"},{"id":"R2","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"partial","where":"Mission Design - Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R9","met":"partial","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"partial","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":"Contents"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Mission Design - Launch"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit needs RAAN to precess about 360° per year (about 0.9856°/day eastward), which the text states as 1° per day and mixes with a full rotation per year; it also says RAAN is 'the initial value indicates the location of the launch facility', which is not how RAAN is defined."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Argument of latitude is described as the angle from ascending node, but the table gives u=0° while stating it changes with position, an internal inconsistency; the Keplerian element table lists only five elements, omitting true anomaly/mean anomaly."},{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","issue":"Launch from Kourou at 5.05°N is said to be used for a retrograde near-polar orbit with a 'piggyback' ride, yet the text then selects a dedicated Vega E; the claim that Kourou's latitude below target inclination gives two windows per day is only loosely justified."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"Claims a polar ground station maximises pass frequency and then selects Tasmania (about 42°S) without quantifying the trade-off; the 'verticality' claim about contact duration is unsupported."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"Drag sail paragraph is truncated mid-sentence; the uncontrolled descent option is said to be chosen but then rejected for lack of thrust without a re-entry or lifetime calculation."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"Altitude of 600 km was chosen by swath argument but the swath width and ground resolution are never calculated; the reef width of 250 km is not related to sensor swath numerically."}],"overall":"Solid concept with STK access data and an orbit table, but mission design contains several technical inconsistencies (RAAN precession, launch vehicle choice, ground station logic), missing figure numbering, a truncated EOL paragraph, and a prohibited Wikipedia source. Adequate overall with strengths in concept and communications results.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Accomplished","mark":8.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Primary mission, rationale, single-satellite justification and users (operator/customers) are addressed with reasonable detail and some references. Operator is vaguely stated (university or government) and referencing is partly via Wikipedia-free web links but lacks depth; still largely accomplished.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Coral reefs harbour some of the most diverse species of life on the planet"},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"Covers launch, orbit with COE table, STK access tables and ground track screenshots. However, launch is inconsistent (piggyback vs dedicated Vega E, Vega E not yet built), RAAN and sun-synchronous logic contain errors, the 'retrograde' vs 'Vega launch from near equator' reasoning is muddled, and manoeuvre/EOL is weakly supported by calculations. Ground station pole-proximity claim contradicts Tasmania choice rationale. Adequate range.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"the satellite will be positioned at an altitude of 600km above Earth's surface"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"An average contact duration of almost ten minutes was achieved"},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Earth radius of 6371km plus a proposed altitude of 600km"}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"Conclusions are reached and tie back to the mission, with acknowledged launch risk, but they are largely generic and do not summarise quantitative results such as the EOL decision or the access statistics in detail.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A specific launch vehicle – the Vega E – has been selected although it has not been built yet."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans"}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"Clear headings and a contents list, but the drag sail sentence is truncated, the Contents page numbers do not match, figures and tables lack proper numbering and titles, the executive summary is long, and a Wikipedia source is used despite being prohibited. Grammar errors appear throughout.","evidence":[{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"However, the ZYZZsat is without thrust capability and so must use a ‘passive’ strategy."},{"where":"Mission Design - Launch","quote":"Vega E"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
